%S A401769 4,6,12,18,30,42,60,72,108,150,180,192,198,228,240,270,312,420,432,
%T A401769 462,522,570,600,660,810,828,858,882,1020,1032,1050,1092,1152,1230,
%U A401769 1290,1302,1320,1428,1452,1488,1620,1722,1872,1932,1950,1998,2028,2088,2112,2142
%N A401769 Averages of twin prime pairs that are 2-dense numbers A174973.
%C A401769 Also averages of twin prime pairs (p, q) such that both the symmetric representation of sigma(p) and the symmetric representation of sigma(q) do not touch (see example).
%C A401769 The smallest average of twin prime pairs not in the sequence is 102.
%C A401769 First differs from A258838 at n = 18.
%H A401769 James C. McMahon, <a href="/A401769/b401769.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%e A401769 6 is in the sequence because it is the average of the twin prime pair (5, 7) and because the divisors of 6 [1, 2, 3, 6] increase by a factor of at most 2.
%e A401769 Also 6 is in the sequence because it is the average of the twin prime pair (5, 7) and both the symmetric representation of sigma(5) = 6 and the symmetric representation of sigma(7) = 8 do not touch, as shown below:
%e A401769 .
%e A401769    _ _ _ _
%e A401769   |_ _ _ _|
%e A401769    _ _ _  |_
%e A401769   |_ _ _|   |_ _
%e A401769         |_ _  | |
%e A401769           | | | |
%e A401769           | | | |
%e A401769           |_| |_|
%e A401769            5   7
%e A401769 .
%t A401769 Select[Prime[Range[323]],PrimeQ[#+2]&&Max[Ratios[Divisors[#+1]]]<=2&]+1 (* _James C. McMahon_, Nov 12 2026 *)
%o A401769 (PARI) is2dense(n) = my(d=divisors(n)); for(i=2, #d, if(d[i] > 2*d[i-1], return(0))); 1;
%o A401769 isok(n) = isprime(n-1) && isprime(n+1) && is2dense(n); \\ _Jeff Sponaugle_, Nov 07 2026
%Y A401769 Intersection of A014574 and A174973.
%Y A401769 The union of this sequence and A400848 gives A014574.
